class.description:
The [JSON] class enables all data types to be converted to and from a JSON string. This is useful for serializing data, e.g. to save to a file or send over the network.
[method stringify] is used to convert any data type into a JSON string.
[method parse] is used to convert any existing JSON data into a [Variant] that can be used within Godot. If successfully parsed, use [member data] to retrieve the [Variant], and use [method @GlobalScope.typeof] to check if the Variant's type is what you expect. JSON Objects are converted into a [Dictionary], but JSON data can be used to store [Array]s, numbers, [String]s and even just a boolean.
[codeblock]
var data_to_send = ["a", "b", "c"]
var json_string = JSON.stringify(data_to_send)
# Save data
# ...
# Retrieve data
var json = JSON.new()
var error = json.parse(json_string)
if error == OK:
	var data_received = json.data
	if typeof(data_received) == TYPE_ARRAY:
		print(data_received) # Prints the array.
	else:
		print("Unexpected data")
else:
	print("JSON Parse Error: ", json.get_error_message(), " in ", json_string, " at line ", json.get_error_line())
[/codeblock]
Alternatively, you can parse strings using the static [method parse_string] method, but it doesn't handle errors.
[codeblock]
var data = JSON.parse_string(json_string) # Returns null if parsing failed.
[/codeblock]
A class can customize its own encoding and decoding by conforming to the [code]JsonSerializable[/code] trait, which declares [code]to_json() -> JsonNode[/code] and [code]static from_json(node: JsonNode) -> JsonResult[Self][/code]. [method stringify] calls [code]to_json()[/code] on any conforming value it encounters, at the top level, as an element of an [Array], or as a [Dictionary] [i]value[/i]; a conforming object used as a [Dictionary] [i]key[/i] does not go through [code]to_json()[/code], since keys are always converted to plain [String]s directly. [method parse_to_node] is the counterpart that hands a parsed document to [code]from_json()[/code].
A conforming type that nests another conforming type composes by explicit delegation, not by any automatic mechanism: the parent's [code]to_json()[/code] calls the child's [code]to_json()[/code] directly, and the parent's [code]from_json()[/code] calls the child's [code]from_json()[/code] and forwards a failure with [code]JsonResult.nested(error, "key")[/code], which re-roots the reported error path at the document instead of at the nested value.
[codeblock]
class_name Player extends Node uses JsonSerializable

var weapon: Weapon

func to_json() -> JsonNode:
	return JsonNode.object_of({"weapon": weapon.to_json()})

static func from_json(node: JsonNode) -> JsonResult[Player]:
	match node:
		JsonNode.Object(var entries):
			var weapon_result := Weapon.from_json(entries.get("weapon", JsonNode.Null))
			if not weapon_result.is_ok():
				return JsonResult[Player].nested(weapon_result.error, "weapon")
			var player := Player.new()
			player.weapon = weapon_result.value
			return JsonResult[Player].ok(player)
		_:
			return JsonResult[Player].fail("expected an object", "$")
[/codeblock]
A conformance can also be declared retroactively on an engine class from outside its definition, and [method stringify] honors it even though the objects it applies to carry no script. The witness is resolved through the engine inheritance chain, so a conformance declared on a base class also covers its subclasses, and an implementation an object already carries itself takes precedence over an external one.
[codeblock]
extend Resource uses JsonSerializable:
    func to_json() -> JsonNode:
        return JsonNode.object_of({"path": JsonNode.Str(resource_path)})

    static func from_json(_node: JsonNode) -> JsonResult[Self]:
        return JsonResult[Self].fail("not decodable", "$")
[/codeblock]
[b]Note:[/b] Custom marshaling has one known limitation. Enums, tuples, and builtin value types are never marshaled through [code]to_json()[/code]/[code]from_json()[/code]: a tagged union or tuple value is a read-only [Array] and a plain enum is an [int] at runtime, so neither carries the type identity [method stringify] needs to dispatch on.
[b]Note:[/b] Both parse methods do not fully comply with the JSON specification:
- Trailing commas in arrays or objects are ignored, instead of causing a parser error.
- New line and tab characters are accepted in string literals, and are treated like their corresponding escape sequences [code]\n[/code] and [code]\t[/code].
- Numbers are parsed using [method String.to_float] which is generally more lax than the JSON specification.
- Certain errors, such as invalid Unicode sequences, do not cause a parser error. Instead, the string is cleaned up and an error is logged to the console.

method.description:
Attempts to parse the [param json_text] provided and returns a [code]JsonResult[JsonNode][/code]: either the parsed document as a [code]JsonNode[/code] tree, or the error explaining why there is none. Unlike [method parse_string], a parse failure is carried in the returned value instead of being reported as [code]null[/code].
Pass the parsed tree to a type's [code]from_json()[/code] to decode it, which is the counterpart of the [code]to_json()[/code] that [method stringify] honors for a value conforming to the [code]JsonSerializable[/code] trait.
[codeblock]
var parsed := JSON.parse_to_node('{"level": 3}')
if parsed.is_ok():
	var player := Player.from_json(parsed.value)
[/codeblock]
[b]Note:[/b] JSON has a single number type, so a number with no fractional part is reported as an [code]Int[/code] node and any other number as a [code]Float[/code] node. These are kept as two separate cases rather than one: folding them together would force every whole number through the [code]Float[/code] case, so decoding a [code skip-lint]hp: int[/code] field would either lose the value's [code skip-lint]int[/code] type or print a whole number like [code]1.0[/code] instead of [code]1[/code]. A decoder for a [code skip-lint]float[/code] field should accept both cases, as most JSON decoders do. Numbers are parsed as doubles, so a literal too large to be held exactly is rounded before it reaches the tree.
[b]Note:[/b] The [code]JsonNode[/code] and [code]JsonResult[/code] types are provided by the scripting language. Calling this method fails when no scripting module supplies them.

Converts a [Variant] var to JSON text and returns the result. Useful for serializing data to store or send over the network.
[b]Note:[/b] An object that conforms to the [code]JsonSerializable[/code] trait is encoded through its own [code]to_json()[/code] instead of the default conversion below; see the class description for how to declare and compose it.
[b]Note:[/b] The JSON specification does not define integer or float types, but only a [i]number[/i] type. Therefore, converting a Variant to JSON text will convert all numerical values to [float] types.
[b]Note:[/b] If [param full_precision] is [code]true[/code], when stringifying floats, the unreliable digits are stringified in addition to the reliable digits to guarantee exact decoding.
The [param indent] parameter controls if and how something is indented; its contents will be used where there should be an indent in the output. Even spaces like [code]"   "[/code] will work. [code]\t[/code] and [code]\n[/code] can also be used for a tab indent, or to make a newline for each indent respectively.
[b]Warning:[/b] Non-finite numbers are not supported in JSON. Any occurrences of [constant @FoundryScript.INF] will be replaced with [code]1e99999[/code], and negative [constant @FoundryScript.INF] will be replaced with [code]-1e99999[/code], but they will be interpreted correctly as infinity by most JSON parsers. [constant @FoundryScript.NAN] will be replaced with [code]null[/code], and it will not be interpreted as NaN in JSON parsers. If you expect non-finite numbers, consider passing your data through [method from_native] first.
[b]Example output:[/b]
[codeblock]
## JSON.stringify(my_dictionary)
{"name":"my_dictionary","version":"1.0.0","entities":[{"name":"entity_0","value":"value_0"},{"name":"entity_1","value":"value_1"}]}

## JSON.stringify(my_dictionary, "\t")
{
	"name": "my_dictionary",
	"version": "1.0.0",
	"entities": [
		{
			"name": "entity_0",
			"value": "value_0"
		},
		{
			"name": "entity_1",
			"value": "value_1"
		}
	]
}

## JSON.stringify(my_dictionary, "...")
{
..."name": "my_dictionary",
..."version": "1.0.0",
..."entities": [
......{
........."name": "entity_0",
........."value": "value_0"
......},
......{
........."name": "entity_1",
........."value": "value_1"
......}
...]
}
[/codeblock]
